@* The guide to the Grammar Tools page: the page's own "?" buttons go deeper into the score and each table. *@
<p class="lead">
    Grammar Tools is where the grammar is changed and scored. Every other page shows the corpus as a grammar reads it;
    this one holds the grammar itself - its glyphs and vocabularies - and measures each change against the corpus
    before it is kept. It is the page to open when the question is <strong>"is this grammar getting better?"</strong>
</p>

<h4>Workspaces</h4>
<p>
    A <strong>workspace</strong> is one grammar, scored against the same corpus as the others. The <em>source</em> workspace is the grammar
    compiled from the C# glyph sources; a <em>scratch</em> workspace is kept as JSON, for experiments that should not touch the sources.
    The switcher on the top row chooses which one every page shows. Here a workspace can be created (empty, or with the current
    vocabularies kept), merged into another, renamed, deleted or exported.
</p>

<h4>Working and committed</h4>
<p>
    Each workspace has a <strong>working</strong> definition, the one being edited, and a <strong>committed</strong> one (a <em>checkpoint</em>, in a
    scratch workspace): the last state kept. The score section shows both and the change between them. Every edit is a
    <strong>step</strong> in the history, which can be undone, redone, or reverted - one step, a round of the agent's, or all of them.
    <em>Commit to C#</em> writes the working definition back into the glyph source files, changing only the declarations that changed;
    <em>Checkpoint</em> marks the working state of a scratch workspace as its new baseline.
</p>

<h4>The score</h4>
<p>
    The score is a <strong>minimum description length</strong>: how many bits it takes to write the whole corpus down using the grammar,
    with the grammar's own definition counted in. Lower is better. A glyph pays for itself when the text it captures is cheaper to
    write as "this glyph, these values" than as raw words, and the grammar's definition is what keeps a one-literal-per-document
    grammar from scoring well. Coverage is reported alongside; the score chooses <em>how</em> to cover text, coverage says
    <em>whether</em> it is. The <strong>?</strong> beside the section goes through the arithmetic with the current figures.
</p>

<h4>The tables</h4>
<dl class="terms">
    <dt>Glyphs</dt>
    <dd>One row per glyph: its net bits (what its captures save, less what its definition costs), its occurrences, and the words it covers. Open a row to edit the glyph's source; <em>New glyph</em> adds one. Each section's <strong>?</strong> explains its columns.</dd>
    <dt>Vocabularies</dt>
    <dd>One row per enum: how many of its members the corpus uses, what its definition costs, and which glyphs use it.</dd>
    <dt>Residual hot spots</dt>
    <dd>The unmatched text that costs the most, ranked: whole spans, and recurring phrases - the next glyphs to write.</dd>
</dl>

<h4>The agent</h4>
<p>
    With a local agent installed, the chat pane runs it over this same grammar: it drafts glyphs, evaluates them against the
    corpus and applies the ones that pay, checking in as the session's settings say. Its rounds appear in the history as the
    page's own steps do, with their time, tokens and cost, and can be reverted as a round. The same tools are served to an
    outside agent at <code>/mcp</code>, so a terminal session and this pane edit one working definition.
</p>

<h4>Definitions</h4>
<p>
    A glyph's definition can be viewed as C#, as JSON, as its formatted or minified regex, or as a diff against the committed
    version. Hovering a glyph's name anywhere in the app previews it in the view chosen in the settings dialog; clicking opens the viewer.
</p>

<div class="callout caution">
    <span class="callout-label">Two rules the apply button keeps</span>
    <p>A step must cover at least one more word, and must not take words from another glyph. A change that breaks either - a refactor, a glyph replacing a wider one - needs the viewer's approval, which the page asks for.</p>
</div>
